  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Heat and humidity continues Friday. Break in the\nheat late in the weekend, but heat builds again towards the \nmiddle of next week.\n\nMuch of the southeastern US and northward into the Mid Atlantic are \nunder the influence of a strong 500mb ridge, allowing the heat and \nhumidity to continue today and through tomorrow. High temperatures \nwill reach the mid to upper 90s in the Piedmont today, potentially \nreaching 100 degrees for some locations, which is 10 to 17 degrees \nabove seasonal normals. Heat indices will range from  100 to 105 \ntoday, though any cloud cover and precipitation could prevent \nreaching the upper end of that range. A Heat Advisory remains in \neffect for the VA and NC Piedmont through this evening, and one may \nbe considered for tomorrow as well, but with some uncertainty in \nhigh temps given the possibility for storms and cloud cover, \nconfidence is not as high in reaching advisory criteria at this time.\n\nFollowing a frontal passage Saturday, Sunday and Monday will be \ncooler, with high temperatures in the mid 70s in the west and low to \nmid 80s in the east, only a couple degrees above normal. Guidance is \nstarting to indicate a wedge pattern setting up over the region, \nwhich typically keeps the area cooler and cloudy, though depends how \nfar south the front moves. Could see temps for Sunday trend a degree \nor two lower in subsequent forecasts. \n\nBy the start of the next work week, a 500mb ridge over the \ncentral US begins to get suppressed by a trough tracking from \nthe Pacific NW towards the northern Plains. The ridge will \nbecome more centered over the southern and eastern US, though \nsome models show some degree of troughing lingering over the \nnortheast. Nonetheless, rising heights underneath the expanding \nridge will lead to increasing temperatures, pushing above normal\nonce again, for the middle part of the week. NBM probabilities \nfor max temperatures 90 degrees or higher are 50% to 75% for \nparts of the VA and NC Piedmont Wednesday, and 70% to 80% by \nThursday.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Strong to severe thunderstorms possible Friday \nand Saturday.\n\nThe hot and humid airmass over the region will contribute to \ninstability supportive of thunderstorms the next several days. \nStrongest bulk shear remains north of area tonight, but\nexpecting shear to increase as ridge breaks down Friday into\nSaturday. Can't rule out stray storm tonight as outflow driven\nthunderstorms skirt our northern CWA. Better opportunity is\nexpected Friday into Saturday. \n\nAs the upper ridge of high pressure breaks down, expect a\nsurface cold front to slide south from the Maso-Dixon Friday\nevening which will provide better forcing for ascent for \ndeep convection. An upper shortwave will move into the area \nSaturday, which again gives additional support for thunderstorm \ndevelopment. Damaging wind gusts are the most likely threat, \nwith isolated hail and heavy rain.",
